\fBXmBool \fBdisabled\fR\fR);
\fBXmBool \fBXmLogFileDisableGet\fP\fR(\fBvoid\fR);
\fBvoid \fBXmLogFunctionDisableSet\fP\fR(
\fBXmBool \fBdisabled\fR\fR);
\fBXmBool \fBXmLogFunctionDisableGet\fP\fR(\fBvoid\fR);
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
These functions form a small logging facility in \fBlibXm\fP, which
applications can use for their own diagnostics\&. Messages are sent to
\fIdomains\fP, each of which has a name, an optional color and a
level, and are printed through a replaceable print callback\&. Motif
itself does not log through this interface; its warnings still go
through \fBXtAppWarningMsg\fP(3)\&.
.SS "Levels"
.PP
A message has one of the levels of the \fBXmLogLevel\fP enumeration,
from the most to the least severe:
\fBXM_LOG_LEVEL_CRITICAL\fP (0), \fBXM_LOG_LEVEL_ERR\fP (1),
\fBXM_LOG_LEVEL_WARN\fP (2), \fBXM_LOG_LEVEL_INFO\fP (3) and
\fBXM_LOG_LEVEL_DBG\fP (4)\&. \fBXM_LOG_LEVEL_UNKNOWN\fP (\-1) is
returned for an unknown domain\&. A message is printed when its level
is less than or equal to the level of its domain and the level of the
domain is not 0\&.
.PP
The default level is set when Motif is built, with the CMake option
\fBLOG_LEVEL\fP (\fBDEBUG\fP, \fBINFO\fP, \fBWARN\fP, \fBERROR\fP or
\fBCRITICAL\fP; \fBINFO\fP by default)\&.
\fBXmLogLevelSet\fP changes this default level, which new domains
start with, and the level of the global domain; existing domains keep
their level\&. \fBXmLogLevelGet\fP returns it\&.
.SS "Initialization"
.PP
\fBXmLogInit\fP selects the print callback named by the build option
\fBLOG_OUTPUT\fP (\fBstderr\fP, the default, \fBstdout\fP or
\fBfile\fP, which appends to \fBmotif\&.log\fP in the current
directory) and registers the global domain \fB"XM"\fP, whose identifier
it stores in the variable \fBXM_LOG_DOMAIN_GLOBAL\fP\&. It must be
called before any message is printed\&. Call it only once\&.
.PP
\fBXmLogShutdown\fP frees all domains\&. Domain identifiers obtained
before it are invalid afterwards\&.
.SS "Domains"
.PP
\fBXmLogDomainRegister\fP registers a domain called \fIname\fP and
returns its identifier, a small non-negative integer, or \-1 if
\fIname\fP is NULL or memory is exhausted\&. \fIcolor\fP is a terminal
escape sequence (for example \fBLOG_COLOR_GREEN\fP) printed before the
domain name, or NULL; the string is not copied and must stay valid
while the domain exists\&. A new domain starts at the default level\&.
.PP
\fBXmLogDomainUnregister\fP removes a domain\&. Its identifier may be
reused by a later registration\&.
.PP
\fBXmLogDomainLevelSet\fP and \fBXmLogDomainLevelGet\fP set and return
the level of the first registered domain called \fIdomain_name\fP;
\fBXmLogDomainRegisteredLevelSet\fP and
\fBXmLogDomainRegisteredLevelGet\fP do the same for a domain
identifier\&. The getters return \fBXM_LOG_LEVEL_UNKNOWN\fP for an
unknown domain\&.
.SS "Printing"
.PP
\fBXmLogPrint\fP prints the message formatted from \fIfmt\fP and the
following arguments as by \fBprintf\fP(3), at \fIlevel\fP, to
\fIdomain\fP\&. \fIfile\fP, \fIfnc\fP and \fIline\fP identify the
caller\&. The macros \fBXM_LOG_CRIT\fP, \fBXM_LOG_ERR\fP,
\fBXM_LOG_WARN\fP, \fBXM_LOG_INFO\fP and \fBXM_LOG_DBG\fP print
to the global domain, and \fBXM_LOG_DOM_CRIT\fP through
\fBXM_LOG_DOM_DBG\fP to the domain given as their first argument; they
pass \fB__FILE__\fP, \fB__func__\fP and \fB__LINE__\fP\&. An unknown
or unregistered domain is reported on standard error instead\&.
.PP
The print callbacks \fBXmLogPrintCbStdErr\fP and
\fBXmLogPrintCbStdOut\fP write a line of the form
.PP
.nf
\fILVL\fP<\fIpid\fP>:\fIdomain\fP \fIfile\fP:\fIline\fP \fIfunction\fP() \fImessage\fP
.fi
.PP
to standard error or standard output, where \fILVL\fP is one of
\fBCRI\fP, \fBERR\fP, \fBWRN\fP, \fBINF\fP and \fBDBG\fP\&. The level,
and the domain when it has a color, are wrapped in terminal color
escape sequences\&. \fBXmLogPrintCbFile\fP writes the same line to the
file whose name is its \fIdata\fP argument: it opens the file for
appending, writes, and closes it again for every message\&.
.PP
\fBXmLogPrintCbSet\fP makes \fIcb\fP the print callback, which is
passed \fIdata\fP with every message; a NULL \fIcb\fP selects
\fBXmLogPrintCbStdErr\fP\&. \fBXmLogSetOutput\fP selects one of the
three callbacks above by name: \fIoutput\fP is \fB"stderr"\fP,
\fB"stdout"\fP or \fB"file"\fP\&. For \fB"file"\fP, \fIfilename\fP
names the file (\fBmotif\&.log\fP when NULL); the string is not copied
and must stay valid while it is in use\&.
.PP
\fBXmLogLevelColorGet\fP returns the color escape sequence used for
\fIlevel\fP, or an empty string for an invalid level\&.
.SS "Threads"
.PP
\fBXmLogThreadsInit\fP records the calling thread as the main thread
and \fBXmLogThreadsEnable\fP marks thread support as enabled (calling
\fBXmLogThreadsInit\fP first if needed)\&. \fBXmLogThreadsMainCheck\fP
returns \fBXM_TRUE\fP when thread support is not enabled or when
called from the main thread\&. \fBXmLogThreadsShutdown\fP, which must
be called from the main thread, disables thread support again\&.
.SS "Obsolete functions"
.PP
\fBXmLogColorDisableSet\fP, \fBXmLogFileDisableSet\fP and
\fBXmLogFunctionDisableSet\fP do nothing, and
\fBXmLogColorDisableGet\fP, \fBXmLogFileDisableGet\fP and
\fBXmLogFunctionDisableGet\fP always return \fBXM_FALSE\fP\&. They are
kept for binary compatibility\&.
.SH "RETURN"
.PP
\fBXmLogInit\fP returns \fBXM_FALSE\fP if the global domain cannot be
registered and \fBXM_TRUE\fP otherwise; \fBXmLogShutdown\fP always
returns \fBXM_TRUE\fP\&. \fBXmLogSetOutput\fP returns \fBXM_FALSE\fP
for an unknown \fIoutput\fP\&. The other return values are described
above\&.
.SH "NOTES"
.PP
The logging functions do not lock anything: they must not be called
from several threads at the same time, and
\fBXmLogThreadsEnable\fP does not change that\&. Messages are printed
with the colors described above whatever the destination, including
files\&.
.SH "EXAMPLE"
.PP
.nf
#include <Xm/Log\&.h>

int dom;

XmLogInit();
dom = XmLogDomainRegister("myapp", LOG_COLOR_CYAN);
XmLogDomainRegisteredLevelSet(dom, XM_LOG_LEVEL_DBG);
XM_LOG_DOM_INFO(dom, "loaded %d items", n);
XmLogShutdown();
.fi
.SH "RELATED"
.PP
\fBXtAppWarningMsg\fP(3) and \fBprintf\fP(3)\&.
